            <ArticleTitle>Dissociative anaesthesia for coronary artery bypass surgery using ketamine and midazolam. A case report.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>624-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Intravenous anaesthesia with a combination of ketamine (Ketalar) and midazolam (Dormicum) infusions was administered to a 49-year-old patient, with a documented history of opiate allergy, undergoing coronary artery bypass surgery. A ketamine infusion supplemented with indomethacin suppositories was used for postoperative pain control.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
